                      %mn 3
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 3
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mn 4
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mn 5
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c = 4/5
              two possible ‘isotopes’ arise. In the second, corresponding to the critical 3-state Potts model, not all the scaling dimensions allowed by BPZ in fact occur, but some of those that do actually appear twice. In fact the constraint of unitarity is not sufficient to determine exactly which representations actually occur in a given CFT. The answer to this is provided by demanding consistency of the theory on a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/torus"} torus
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+form"} modular
              transformation, and the requirement of modular invariance has become another powerful tool in classifying CFTs, completely solved in the case
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo &lt;
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c \lt 1
              by Cappelli, Itzykson and Zuber.
          %p
            Some relationships between columns can be identified. For example, dynamical surface growth and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/random+matrix+theory"} random matrix theory
            are, unexpectedly, linked: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/eigenvalue"} eigenvalue
            distributions of the
            %span.newWikiWord
              Gaussian Unitary
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Gaussian+Unitary+Ensemble"} ?
            and
            %span.newWikiWord
              Gaussian Orthogonal Ensembles
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Gaussian+Orthogonal+Ensemble"} ?
            show up as surface heights in Kardar-Parisi-Zhang phenomena. See (
            %a{:href => "#Takeuchi"}>
              Takeuchi
              %em et al.
            ).
          %h2#timeline Timeline
          %p
            The modern terminology of this subject dates to the late 1960s and early ’70s. By 1968, the quantitative concurrence of
            = succeed "s" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                critical exponent
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/critical+exponent"} ?
            = succeed ";" do
              %a{:href => "#Fisher1968"} Fisher 1968
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Sethna2006"} Sethna 2006
          .standout
            %p
              All phase transition problems can be divided into a small number of different classes depending upon the dimensionality of the system and the symmetries of the order state. Within each class, all phase transitions have identical behaviour in the critical region, only the names of the variables are changed. (
              %a{:href => "#Kadanoff1971"}> Kadanoff 1971
              )
          %p The following is a capsule history tabulating some important events in the historical development of the modern understanding of universality.
          %table
            %thead
              %tr
                %th Year
                %th
                %th Contributors
                %th
                %th Event
            %tbody
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1733
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} de Moivre
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Suggestion of what we now know as the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/central+limit+theorem"} central limit theorem
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1822
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Cagniard de la Tour
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Observation of critical point at end of liquid-gas coexistence curve
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1873–75
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} van der Waals, Maxwell
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Equation of state for non-ideal gases, and its application to phase transitions
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1893
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} van der Waals
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Use of critical exponents to characterize a phase transition (
                  %a{:href => "#LeveltSengers1976"}> Levelt Sengers 1976
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1895
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} P. Curie
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Experimental observation of demagnetization by heat; pointing out similarity between liquid-gas and ferromagnetic transitions
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1896–1900
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Verschaffelt
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Discovery that the critical exponents predicted from van der Waals theory do not match experiment (
                  %a{:href => "#LeveltSengers1976"}> Levelt Sengers 1976
                  )
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1901
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Lyapunov
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Demonstration of why the central limit theorem works
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1908
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Smoluchowski
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Discovery that critical opalescence is due to density fluctuations across many scales
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1937
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Landau
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} General mean-field theory of phase transitions
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1944
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Onsager
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Exact solution of 2D
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ising+model"} Ising model
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1947–49
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Feynman, Schwinger, Tomonaga
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Workable
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/renormalization"} renormalization
                  techniques for “sweeping infinities under the rug” in
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+electrodynamics"} quantum electrodynamics
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1954–56
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Stueckelberg, Gell-Mann, Bogoliubov
                  %em et al.
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Discovery that the “renormalization group” (RG) captured an important formal property of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1955
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Wigner
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Eigenvalue statistics of random matrices
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1970–72
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                  Kadanoff, Wilson
                  %em et al.
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Development of RG concept, discovery that it explains universal properties of phase transitions like critical exponents
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1976
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Doi
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Second-quantization formalism for classical stochastic many-particle dynamics
              %tr
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} 1976–79
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"}
                %td{:style => "text-align: left;"} Feigenbaum
